    "answer": {
      "short": "$60^{\\circ}=\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$ radians. Since $\\pi>3$, this is greater than one radian.",
      "worked": "An inscribed regular hexagon has perimeter $6r$, strictly less than the circle's circumference $2\\pi r$. Hence $\\pi>3$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}60^{\\circ}&=60\\times\\frac{\\pi}{180}\\text{ radians}\\\\60^{\\circ}&=\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\text{ radians}.\\end{align*}$$\nSince $\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}>1$, one radian is less than $60^{\\circ}$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 40
  },
